            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Learn to [[Solve-a-Problem|solve problems]] yourself. Children who are seen as \"bad\" because of bad behavior often have trouble dealing with their problems in the proper way.  Confusion and frustration often lead to bad decisions. But being able to recognize and solve problems puts you on a path toward self-reliance and confidence.<ref>https://raisingchildren.net.au/pre-teens/behaviour/encouraging-good-behaviour/problem-solving-steps</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Learn to [[Solve-a-Problem|solve problems]] yourself. Children who are seen as \"bad\" because of bad behavior often have trouble dealing with their problems in the proper way.  Confusion and frustration often lead to bad decisions. But being able to recognize and solve problems puts you on a path toward self-reliance and confidence.<ref>https://raisingchildren.net.au/pre-teens/behaviour/encouraging-good-behaviour/problem-solving-steps</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "33145597",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "23:23, 12 February 2025",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,524 bytes)",
            "change": 26,
            "summary": "(restoring prev vis for stu)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Accept your [[Be-Responsible|responsibilities]]. It is easy to say that a good child listens to their parents (and other authority figures) and does what they are told. While this is normally true, it is more important that children learn to take responsibility for what they need to do. As a child who strives to be your best, you need to accept that there are things you have to do, for the benefit of yourself and others.<ref>https://psychcentral.com/blog/imperfect/2020/01/when-kids-have-to-act-like-adults</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Manage your emotions. Every one of us (including adults) sometimes gets angry, frustrated, whiny, or stressed out. There is no way to deny or avoid these emotions and it would be unhealthy to do so anyway. However, you can work on [[Understand-Your-Emotions|recognizing]] and managing your emotions more effectively.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/intl/blog/blamestorming/201708/how-manage-your-emotions</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Be [[Be-Honest|honest]] and trusting.  “Good boys and girls tell the truth.”  You may have heard this said to you, and it is usually true.  But the bigger picture is that honesty is a key part of building relationships based on trust.  This will benefit you as a child and as an adult.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/intl/blog/happiness-in-world/201402/why-be-honest</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Expect imperfection and [[Accept-Mistakes-and-Learn-from-Them|learn from your mistakes]].  Even the best kids make plenty of mistakes.  It is part of growing up, and simply of being human.  The important thing is what you do with your imperfections. Learning from mistakes is a sign of [[Be-a-Mature-Teenager|maturity]] and is sure to be appreciated by your parents.[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn to [[Solve-a-Problem|solve problems]] yourself. Children who are seen as \"bad\" because of bad behavior often have trouble dealing with their problems in the proper way.  Confusion and frustration often lead to bad decisions. But being able to recognize and solve problems puts you on a path toward self-reliance and confidence.<ref>https://raisingchildren.net.au/pre-teens/behaviour/encouraging-good-behaviour/problem-solving-steps</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Know when to ask for help.  As we just discussed, learning to recognize and solve problems yourself is an important skill for kids (and adults).  But, just as important is being able to recognize and accept when you need help dealing with a problem.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/tracking-wonder/202002/4-tips-effectively-ask-help-and-get-yes</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Treat others like you want to be treated.  Many people call this the “golden rule,” and it really is a valuable rule to live by.  For children, acting toward your parents, friends and family, and other people with this guide in mind demonstrates thoughtfulness and maturity on your part.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/the-heart-and-science-attachment/202108/why-we-treat-others-we-have-been-treated</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn to recognize how other people are feeling.  If you know how other people are feeling and are likely to react, you will have a great advantage in deciding how you should behave in that situation. For instance, if your parents are stressed out about how they are going to pay the bills for the month, it is probably not the best time to ask for a video game or new shoes. Or, if your brother is upset about not making the baseball team, it probably isn’t the best time to rib him about his lack of athletic skills.<ref>https://psychcentral.com/health/reasons-you-and-others-invalidate-your-emotional-experience</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Show concern and compassion.  When someone is hurting, or needs a hand, take it upon yourself to do what you can to help.  The world can always use more compassionate, [[Be-Helpful|helpful]] people.  Why not start when you are still a child?<ref>https://positivepsychology.com/character-strength-kindness/</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Offer gratitude to those who help you.  As you become more aware of how you can help others, you should also become more aware of all the people that help you.  Let them know you appreciate all they do for you.  This is definitely a “good child” quality, and an important part of maturing into a responsible and happy person.<ref>https://www.health.harvard.edu/healthbeat/giving-thanks-can-make-you-happier</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Child Step 10.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Accept your [[Be-Responsible|responsibilities]]. It is easy to say that a good child listens to their parents (and other authority figures) and does what they are told. While this is normally true, it is more important that children learn to take responsibility for what they need to do. As a child who strives to be your best, you need to accept that there are things you have to do, for the benefit of yourself and others.<ref>https://psychcentral.com/blog/imperfect/2020/01/when-kids-have-to-act-like-adults</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Boy Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Manage your emotions. Every one of us (including adults) sometimes gets angry, frustrated, whiny, or stressed out. There is no way to deny or avoid these emotions and it would be unhealthy to do so anyway. However, you can work on [[Understand-Your-Emotions|recognizing]] and managing your emotions more effectively.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/intl/blog/blamestorming/201708/how-manage-your-emotions</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Boy Step 21.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Be [[Be-Honest|honest]] and trusting.  “Good boys and girls tell the truth.”  You may have heard this said to you, and it is usually true.  But the bigger picture is that honesty is a key part of building relationships based on trust.  This will benefit you as a child and as an adult.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/intl/blog/happiness-in-world/201402/why-be-honest</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Kid Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Expect imperfection and [[Accept-Mistakes-and-Learn-from-Them|learn from your mistakes]].  Even the best kids make plenty of mistakes.  It is part of growing up, and simply of being human.  The important thing is what you do with your imperfections. Learning from mistakes is a sign of [[Be-a-Mature-Teenager|maturity]] and is sure to be appreciated by your parents.[[Image:Love Your Kids Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn to [[Solve-a-Problem|solve problems]] yourself. Children who are seen as \"bad\" because of bad behavior often have trouble dealing with their problems in the proper way.  Confusion and frustration often lead to bad decisions. But being able to recognize and solve problems puts you on a path toward self-reliance and confidence.[[Image:Love Your Kids Step 8.jpg|center]]<ref>https://raisingchildren.net.au/pre-teens/behaviour/encouraging-good-behaviour/problem-solving-steps</ref>",
                    "#Know when to ask for help.  As we just discussed, learning to recognize and solve problems yourself is an important skill for kids (and adults).  But, just as important is being able to recognize and accept when you need help dealing with a problem.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/tracking-wonder/202002/4-tips-effectively-ask-help-and-get-yes</ref>[[Image:Obtain Money from Your Parents Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Treat others like you want to be treated.  Many people call this the “golden rule,” and it really is a valuable rule to live by.  For children, acting toward your parents, friends and family, and other people with this guide in mind demonstrates thoughtfulness and maturity on your part.<ref>https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/the-heart-and-science-attachment/202108/why-we-treat-others-we-have-been-treated</ref>[[Image:Be a Good Boy Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn to recognize how other people are feeling.  If you know how other people are feeling and are likely to react, you will have a great advantage in deciding how you should behave in that situation. For instance, if your parents are stressed out about how they are going to pay the bills for the month, it is probably not the best time to ask for a video game or new shoes. Or, if your brother is upset about not making the baseball team, it probably isn’t the best time to rib him about his lack of athletic skills.[[Image:Be a Good Son Step 13.jpg|center]]<ref>https://psychcentral.com/health/reasons-you-and-others-invalidate-your-emotional-experience</ref>",
                    "#Show concern and compassion.  When someone is hurting, or needs a hand, take it upon yourself to do what you can to help.  The world can always use more compassionate, [[Be-Helpful|helpful]] people.  Why not start when you are still a child?[[Image:Get Out of a Punishment Step 5.jpg|center]]<ref>https://positivepsychology.com/character-strength-kindness/</ref>",
                    "#Offer gratitude to those who help you.  As you become more aware of how you can help others, you should also become more aware of all the people that help you.  Let them know you appreciate all they do for you.  This is definitely a “good child” quality, and an important part of maturing into a responsible and happy person.[[Image:Be a Good Son Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]<ref>https://www.health.harvard.edu/healthbeat/giving-thanks-can-make-you-happier</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
